# VM Creation Lab

## Objective

Create and configure virtual machines using KVM/QEMU and libvirt as part of a cybersecurity homelab. The goal was to establish dedicated Linux and Windows environments that can later be used for security testing, administration, monitoring, and network experimentation.

## Host Environment

* **CPU:** AMD Ryzen 7 3700X
* **CPU:** 8 physical cores / 16 logical processors
* **RAM:** 64 GB
* **GPU:** NVIDIA RTX 3070
* **Storage:** 1 TB NVMe
* **Host OS:** Ubuntu Linux
* **Virtualization:** KVM/QEMU
* **VM management:** libvirt

## Virtual Machines

### Ubuntu VM — `lab-ubuntu01`

| Configuration   | Value     |
| --------------- | --------- |
| vCPU            | 2         |
| RAM             | 4 GiB     |
| Virtual disk    | 30 GiB    |
| Disk format     | QCOW2     |
| Virtual NIC     | Virtio    |
| Virtual network | `default` |
| Security model  | AppArmor  |
| Persistent      | Yes       |
| Autostart       | Disabled  |

Virtual disk:

```text
/var/lib/libvirt/images/lab-ubuntu01.qcow2
```

The virtual disk has a capacity of approximately 30 GiB. Because QCOW2 supports sparse allocation, the physical storage consumed by the image is currently smaller than its virtual capacity.

### Windows VM — `lab-windows01`

| Configuration   | Value        |
| --------------- | ------------ |
| vCPU            | 4            |
| RAM             | 8 GiB        |
| Virtual disk    | 80 GiB       |
| Disk format     | QCOW2        |
| Virtual NIC     | Intel E1000e |
| Virtual network | `default`    |
| Security model  | AppArmor     |
| Persistent      | Yes          |
| Autostart       | Disabled     |

Virtual disk:

```text
/var/lib/libvirt/images/lab-windows01.qcow2
```

Installation media:

```text
/var/lib/libvirt/boot/Windows11_Client_x64_en-us_26300_9457.iso
```

The Windows VM was configured with an 80 GiB virtual disk and an attached Windows 11 installation ISO.

## Virtualization Architecture

The physical Ubuntu system acts as the virtualization host. KVM/QEMU provides hardware-assisted virtualization, while libvirt provides management and configuration of the virtual machines.

```text
Physical Ubuntu Host
│
├── KVM/QEMU
│
├── lab-ubuntu01
│   ├── 2 vCPU
│   ├── 4 GiB RAM
│   ├── 30 GiB QCOW2
│   └── Virtio NIC
│
└── lab-windows01
    ├── 4 vCPU
    ├── 8 GiB RAM
    ├── 80 GiB QCOW2
    └── E1000e NIC
```

Both virtual machines are attached to the libvirt `default` virtual network. Detailed network and LAN configuration is documented separately in the networking section.

## Security Considerations

Virtualization provides separate operating-system environments on the same physical host. This allows the lab to isolate systems and conduct security-related experiments without requiring separate physical computers.

The VMs use QCOW2 virtual disks, allowing the virtual storage to be managed independently from the host operating system.

The VMs are also protected by AppArmor at the libvirt virtualization layer.

Network configuration is intentionally documented separately to distinguish **VM creation and resource allocation** from **network architecture and connectivity**.

